#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279151 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a418d9aa-d74d-58b1-a4c9-819f63593d50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given the Value of Their Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b413d0d-adcc-5ede-88f1-f525f73eea61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75.63}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279153} {:answer/id #uuid "e7607c45-5946-5e09-8e46-93b4cbfdf214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{77.21}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279154} {:answer/id #uuid "a371afc8-0ab5-56aa-9ecd-fcc72a15a54d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{79.84}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279155} {:answer/id #uuid "053f1e8f-6088-5c59-b0e7-80c34f51dd02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{73.42}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279156} {:answer/id #uuid "27f2d286-92ed-577b-a2e7-38b423d4a591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{71.36}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b413d0d-adcc-5ede-88f1-f525f73eea61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75.63}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279153} :answer-field/id #uuid "15ab182e-bbfb-5624-8909-cb1e05f3c135" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "840ed0f5-3459-5b4f-a6cd-56893ad1441d" :question/math-academy-id "q-101517" :question/problem "Consider the points $A(2t,-1,5)$, $B(-3,t,1)$ and $C(2t+1,2,-t).$ Find $∠BAC$ given that $\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}=5.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨2t,\\,-1,\\,5⟩,\\,b=⟨-3,\\,t,\\,1⟩,\\,c=⟨2t+1,\\,2,\\,-t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/assets/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨-3,\\,t,\\,1⟩-⟨2t,\\,-1,\\,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3-2t,\\,t+1,\\,-4⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨2t+1,\\,2,\\,-t⟩-⟨2t,\\,-1,\\,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,3,\\,-t-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}=5$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =5 \\\\ (-3-2t)\\cdot 1+(t+1)\\cdot 3+-4\\cdot (-t-5) & =5 \\\\ -3-2t+3t+3+4t+20 & =5 \\\\ 5t+20 & =5 \\\\ 5t & =-15 \\\\ t & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨-3-2(-3),(-3)+1,-4⟩, \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,-4⟩, \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =1,\\,3,\\,-(-3)-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,3,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-4)^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}\\cdot \\sqrt{14}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{406}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{406}})≈{75.63}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7b1b8dc-bb50-59b4-8716-bdd6fbeada4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Three Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e66664f5-380e-5745-be2c-90ad323f2dfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{46}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279160} {:answer/id #uuid "0714c6d5-fa2e-5d2a-b410-9cc7f6f50f9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{41}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279161} {:answer/id #uuid "726f5315-901e-5c3f-a2a2-290b90bc03b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{38}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279162} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc7251c-e514-5ea7-bf1a-270ab49b97f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{35}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279163} {:answer/id #uuid "0adb7bb9-01cb-5473-8b8b-f7b52effa7b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{50}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0714c6d5-fa2e-5d2a-b410-9cc7f6f50f9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{41}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279161} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f5b0741-3fca-5a95-8d71-75222a5d3195" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2793518d-ad57-5b32-92b4-c966424db7d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-124813" :question/problem "Find $∠BAC$ given the points $A(2,-1,2)$, $B(3,-5,1)$, and $C(1,-4,-1).$ Round your answer to the nearest degree." :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨2,-1,2⟩,\\,b=⟨3,-5,1⟩,\\,c=⟨1,-4,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/assets/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨3,-5,1⟩-⟨2,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-4,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨1,-4,-1⟩-⟨2,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,-3,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-1)+(-4)\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot (-3)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-3)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{\\sqrt{18}\\cdot \\sqrt{19}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3\\sqrt{38}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{14}{3\\sqrt{38}})≈{41}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7b1b8dc-bb50-59b4-8716-bdd6fbeada4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Three Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "696a450b-0b6f-5d21-b2df-f450197f9c10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{58}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279167} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc1a12e-1ec9-5610-8c5d-5006261870cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{70}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279168} {:answer/id #uuid "3e49d3ec-f0aa-5abd-9ded-ed8c9885adeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{85}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279169} {:answer/id #uuid "bc13e402-5e5b-5248-82fd-f71f12f35d1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279170} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ad0921-944f-5bea-ad24-07d4f0f5bfa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{113}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "696a450b-0b6f-5d21-b2df-f450197f9c10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{58}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279167} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7d239a0-5555-5a5f-8e7c-5541a5a59980" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c70e65b8-080c-5852-97fc-a933e5e34270" :question/math-academy-id "q-18322" :question/problem "Find $∠BAC$ given the points $A(1,0,-2)$, $B(1,2,4)$, and $C(3,-2,1).$ Round your answer to the nearest degree." :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨1,0,-2⟩,\\,b=⟨1,2,4⟩,\\,c=⟨3,-2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/assets/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨1,2,4⟩-⟨1,0,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,2,6⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,1⟩-⟨1,0,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-2)+6\\cdot 3}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{6}^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{\\sqrt{40}\\cdot \\sqrt{17}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{\\sqrt{170}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{7}{\\sqrt{170}})≈{58}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106032 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7b1b8dc-bb50-59b4-8716-bdd6fbeada4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Three Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aca6bd26-6933-59e2-8800-0702ffaaee5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102430} {:answer/id #uuid "b59b3837-6fdf-509f-b5ae-fc2925a1d015" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102431} {:answer/id #uuid "bd485a1b-1304-5ab4-ac9e-8d4a2f9d2b36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102432} {:answer/id #uuid "08d4a2e8-a01f-5019-a296-05c07d911af5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102433} {:answer/id #uuid "673c1a77-a57c-5bbd-8e96-8088500a00a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08d4a2e8-a01f-5019-a296-05c07d911af5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102433} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2546906-9052-54aa-8870-1c81e7a0a86b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6f80f82-94f9-5bde-ac4d-44929ef66109" :question/math-academy-id "q-19110" :question/problem "Find $∠QPR$ given the points $P(0,4,5)$, $Q(1,5,4)$, and $R(2,3,6).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨0,4,5⟩,\\,q=⟨1,5,4⟩,\\,r=⟨2,3,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/assets/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨1,5,4⟩-⟨0,4,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨2,3,6⟩-⟨0,4,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)={90}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a418d9aa-d74d-58b1-a4c9-819f63593d50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given the Value of Their Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52d09f0d-ef08-51ed-9676-488cfd7fd686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{84.26}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279174} {:answer/id #uuid "a600b8de-19f2-5c16-8bbb-3db6c5396720" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{64.52}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279175} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d0008a-17c7-5148-a615-14be8fa49411" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{53.13}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279176} {:answer/id #uuid "84bb65f9-fe22-5996-8f58-e03a81a38199" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{66.42}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279177} {:answer/id #uuid "908967fd-9f87-517e-b14e-dd9063dc0012" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{78.46}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84bb65f9-fe22-5996-8f58-e03a81a38199" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{66.42}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279177} :answer-field/id #uuid "70c3f7dc-1cfb-5635-be40-ad2dadddc2e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e38d8e83-96eb-54fa-b9d4-c3057eacb534" :question/math-academy-id "q-77165" :question/problem "Consider the points $A(5,3t+2,2),$ $B(1,3t,2)$ and $C(1,4t,3).$ Find $∠ABC$ given that $\\overset{\\to }{BA}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{BC}=4.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,$ $B$ and $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨5,\\,3t+2,\\,2⟩,\\,b=⟨1,\\,3t,\\,2⟩,\\,c=⟨1,\\,4t,\\,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠ABC=\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/assets/2c81d48afacc4ac5e81ab501bc713009ea4425451fcf8dbf414672aeadbb1679.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{BA}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{BC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BA} & =a-b \\\\  & =⟨5,\\,3t+2,\\,2⟩-⟨1,\\,3t,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,2,\\,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{BC} & =c-b \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,4t,\\,3⟩-⟨1,\\,3t,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,t,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\overset{\\to }{BA}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{BC}=4$, we can solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BA}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{BC} & =4 \\\\ 4\\cdot 0+2\\cdot t+0\\cdot 1 & =4 \\\\ 0+2t+0 & =4 \\\\ 2t & =4 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BA} & =⟨4,2,0⟩, \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{BC} & =⟨0,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{BA}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{BC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{BA}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{BC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{BA}||\\overset{\\to }{BC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{20}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{100}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{2}{5})≈{66.42}^{∘}.\n$$"}]}
